## 1 Introduction
Poly(ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrene sulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS) organic electrochemical transistor (OECT), featuring high electronic and ionic mobilities, volumetric charging with high transconductance $(g_{m})$ , low working voltages $(<1\mathrm{\V})$ and superior biocompatibility [1,2], have shown great promise in biosensing [3], bioelectronics [4], and neuromorphic computing [5,6]. Remarkably, with the ever-growing demands for advanced biomolecular detection, PEDOT:PSS OECT biosensing has gained considerable interests since it has proven to outperform traditional electrochemical technique. For instance, it could response much stronger than the electrode due to its inherent amplification capability [7,8]. As demonstrated in our and other’s previous reports [9–14], PEDOT:PSS OECT biosensing could be operated either on the gate/electrolyte or channel/electrolyte interfaces.
Despite the advantages, one of its major drawbacks is that it works in depletion-mode, in which the bio-relevant electrochemical de-doping of the PEDOT:PSS channel is used to switch the device off [15]. Under such a signal-off operation, the strong background signal is prone to inferior sensitivity of the sensory system. Specifically, the signal reduction faces a maximum capacity of $100\%$ and the small signal change at very low analyte concentration could bias false-positive detection [16], whereas the signal-on one is without those limits. Besides, the depletion-mode PEDOT:PSS OECT of always on state has adverse effect on power consumption [17]. Recent exploration of amine-based molecular de-dopants has been shown to alter the doping state of PEDOT: PSS OECT, which indicated the possibility for generic signal-on PEDOT:PSS OECT biosensing [18,19].
Another major issue of PEDOT:PSS OECT biosensing is the necessity of high gate bias $(V_{\mathrm{g}})$ , of which the long-term application is not beneficial to fragile biomoleculars, e.g., proteins and enzymes that are easy to denature [13]. Minimization or removal of the instrumental $V_{\mathrm{g}}$ is thus desirable as it can reduce the enery comsumption and even save a power supply, which is especially useful in case of strict power and spatial limitations [20]. Our recent bridging with light-harvesting materials has enabled the photoelectrochemical (PEC) [21–25] modulation of the PEDOT:PSS OECT with remote control of the device by light stimulation, and the proposed concept of organic photoelectrochemical transistor (OPECT) has been largely untapped [9,10,26–28]. Bearing in mind the potential and linkage between semiconductor physics and PEDOT:PSS OECT rationales, we hypothesized that photocathodic modulation of amine dedoped PEDOT:PSS might enable the signal-on PEDOT:PSS OECT biosensing, providing the cooperation of proper gate materials.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with unique physicochemical properties have received much attention in interdisciplinary studies [29]. In particular, porphyrinic MOFs (porous coordination networks, PCNs) have recently found increasing applications, e.g., photodynamic therapy [30] and photocatalysis [31], due to its light-harvesting and exciton generation capability. Nevetheless, pristine porphyrinic MOFs could only generate weak currents [32], limiting their potential in many aspects. Hole-transporting layer (HTL) has proved to be an effective route to improve the photo-toelectric efficiency of light-absorbing materials in various optoelectronic devices [33]. On the other hand, transition metal carbides/carbonitrides/nitrides (MXenes) have been shown to be promising materials in electrocatalysis [34] and energy storage [35], due to its excellent electrical conductivity, mechanical properties and biocompatibility [36]. Considering their different energy band and Fermi level, we expected the construction of Schottky junction between specific porphyrinic MOFs and MXenes upon an HTL could produce desirable gate material.
Herein we report such a signal-on PEDOT:PSS OECT biosensing modulated by a MXene/MOFs/NiO Schottky heterojunction, which was exemplified using a diethylenetriamine (DETA) de-doped PEDOT:PSS OECT gated by $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ towards evaluation of enzyme level. As illustrated in Scheme 1a, PCN-224 was prepared by solvothermal treatment of $\mathrm{\zrocl_{4}}$ and tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin $\mathrm{(H_{2}T C P P)}$ , while $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ nanosheets (NSs) were obtained from removal of aluminum (Al) layer and then ultrasonic exfoliation of the multilayer $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ . Then, the asprepared PCN-224 hexahedron and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ NSs were sequentially coupled onto a NiO nanoflakes (NFs) HTL to form the designed Schottky heterostructure on an F-doped tin oxide (FTO) substrate. As shown in Scheme 1b, in linkage with a bi-enzyme cascade system, the quinone derivatives produced by the cascade reaction of alkaline phosphatase (ALP) and tyrosinase (TYR) could serve as effective electron acceptors for the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ heterojunction. To prevent any cross reaction in traditional OECT biosensing, as illustrated in Scheme 1c, the device was operated within a nafion membrane equipped H-cell. Under the light illumination, as shown in Scheme 1d, the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ gate will drive the positive charge away from originally compensated sulfonic acid group in the DETA de-doped PEDOT:PSS, causing an increase of the conductivity. As the gating effect depends intimately upon the quinone derivatives decided by the enzyme level, a unique rationale for biochemical detection could be tailored.
## 2 Results and discussion
As revealed by scanning electron microscope (SEM) in Figure 1a, the as-prepared PCN-224 exhibited a cubic morphology, which was attributed to the formation of wellcrystallized structure consisting of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ clusters linked to square planar TCPP ligands [37]. Figure 1b shows the accordion-like multilayer structure of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ . It was derived from the exfoliation of aluminum layer in $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ , whose morphology was given in Figure S1 (Supporting Information online). Figure 1c shows the ultrathin $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ nanosheets (NSs) obtained by dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) intercalation and ultrasonication of the accordion-like $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ , the thickness of which was determined as ca. 3 nm by atomic force microscopy (AFM), as shown in Figure S2. Figure 1d and inset shows the crossed nanostructure of the employed NiO HTL with a height of ca. $300\mathrm{nm}$ . The composite structure of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ was then developed and characterized as shown in Figure 1e. Remarkably, PCN-224 could be easily observed on the NiO HTL, whereas the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ could not due to its ultrathin film and the high contrast under SEM observation. However, as shown in Figure 1f, the corresponding energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping revealed the clear distribution of the Ni, O, C, Zr and Ti elements, proving the existence of the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ in the asprepared $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}.$ . Such a result could be further confirmed by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) as shown in Figure 1g. The full survey spectrum displays the relevant elements of Ni, Zr, Ti, and C, agreeing well with the EDS result. The corresponding high-resolution XPS spectra of the specific peaks were recorded and discussed in Figure S3. Then, the crystal structures of the as-fabricated PCN224, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ NSs, NiO, and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ were investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD). As shown in Figure 1h, the peaks of PCN-224 at $2\theta$ values of $4.6^{\circ}$ , $6.4^{\circ}$ , $7.9^{\circ}$ , $9.1^{\circ}$ , and $13.7^{\circ}$ were attributed to (002), (022), (222), (004) and (006) crystal facets [37]. The peaks of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ NSs at $2\theta$ values of $8.6^{\circ}$ , $19.1^{\circ}$ , $36.7^{\circ}$ , $41.8^{\circ}$ , and $60.2^{\circ}$ were attributed to (002), (004), (103), (105) and (110) crystal facets [38], while the diffraction peaks of NiO at $2\theta$ values of $37.3^{\circ}$ , $43.3^{\circ}$ , $62.8^{\circ}$ , and $75.3^{\circ}$ corresponded to the (111), (200), (220), and (311) crystal planes (JCPDS Card No. 78-0643), respectively. As expected, the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}{\mathrm C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}.$ 224/NiO kept the predominant diffraction peaks from PCN224, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ NSs, and NiO, further implying the successful integration of the three materials.
To elucidate their optical properties, UV-vis diffuse-reflectance spectra were then recorded. As shown in Figure 2a, PCN-224 exhibited an obvious absorption peak around $425\mathrm{nm}$ and four weak peaks in the region of $500{-}700~\mathrm{nm}$ (green line), which were caused by Q-bands and Soret band of TCPP [39]. $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene displayed no absorption edge in the range from 300 to $800\mathrm{nm}$ because of its metallic property (black line) [40], while NiO had hardly any absorption in the visible region due to its wide band gap (blue line) [41]. Regarding the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ , the spectrum well retained the absorption peak of PCN-224 and thus ensured the light-harvesting capability of the hybrid material (red line). Subsequently, the PEC properties of the samples were characterized by transient photocurrent responses under the intermittent visible light stimulation from a $425\mathrm{nm}$ light source. As shown in Figure 2b, both PCN-224 (green line) and NiO (black line) generated small cathodic photocurrents, while $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ (purple line) exhibited no noticeable photocurrent under the light illumination. If PCN-224 was coupled with the NiO, a larger photocurrent was generated (blue line). Remarkably, after further modification with $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ displayed a significant cathodic response (red line), suggesting the successful synergy among the materials with much enhanced light-to-electricity conversion efficiency. Such a behavior can be attributed to the formation of a Schottky junction within the complex. As shown in Figure 2c, because of the difference between the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and Fermi level (EF), PCN-224 and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ could produce Schottky junctions with a built-in electric field between them [42]. Upon light stimulation, the photoinduced holes would rapidly transmit from the HOMO of PCN-224 to the valence band (VB) of NiO and then be captured by the FTO electrode. Meanwhile, the photoinduced electrons excited to the LUMO from PCN224 would cross the Schottky barrier and then be collected by $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ [42–44], suppressing the recombination of electronhole pairs. Note that the 2D $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ with large surface area and excellent electronic conductivity would not only guarantee the maximal accessibility of the electrolyte to the electrode surface, but also benefit the interfacial charge transfer and diffusion kinetics [45].
As shown in Figure 2d, the PEC stability of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}C_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}.$ 224/NiO was then examined by repeatedly recording the photocurrent every $20~\mathrm{s}$ over $20\mathrm{min}$ , and the nearly unchanged cathodic signals indicated its high photophysical stability. As 1,4-bezoquinone (BQ) could serve as efficient electron acceptor in the electrolyte, its effect on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}C_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}.$ 224/NiO was then studied. As shown in Figure 2e, in the presence of 1 mM BQ, as compared to the cathodic photocurrent of PCN-224/NiO enhanced from ca. 26 to ca. $60\mathrm{nA}$ , the photocurrent of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ significantly increased from ca. 72 to ca. $210\mathrm{nA}$ , indicating the high sensitivity of the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ photocathode to the BQ due to the presence of 2D $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ . Next, the responsibility of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ to the in situ generated quinones was studied by introduction of ALP/TYR bi-enzyme cascade into the system. As displayed in Figure 2f, compared to that of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ electrode (black line), the addition of $50\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ TYR and $2.5\mathrm{mM}$ $O$ -phospho-L-tyrosine (P-Tyr) could not change its response due to the blocked catalytic cascade and thus inhibited quinones generation (blue line). However, further addition of $0.2\mathrm{~U~L~}^{-1}$ ALP could give rise to an obviously enhanced response (red line), because of the continuous generation of quinones under bi-enzyme cascade as illustrated in Scheme 1b. Incidentally, Figure S4 shows the photocurrent responses of $0.2\mathrm{~U~L~}^{-1}$ ALP at different incubation time.
On the other hand, aliphatic amines have been proven to effectively alter the doping state of PEDOT:PSS through the cascade reaction of electrons and protons in the amine [18,19]. Herein DETA was used to de-dope PEDOT:PSS to manufacture accumulation-mode OECT, the optimization of which were included in Figure S5. As shown in Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ and inset, compared with that of pristine PEDOT:PSS OECT (black line), a significantly decreased $\boldsymbol{I}_{\mathrm{D}}$ at $V_{\mathrm{G}}{=}0\mathrm{V}$ indicated the successful fabrication accumulation-mode OECTs (red line). Figure 2h and Figure S6 show the corresponding output characteristic curves of the accumulation-mode OECT and the depletion-mode OECT, respectively. The responses of the accumulation-mode OECT to varying potential steps from 0.1 to $-0.4\:\mathrm{V}$ were then recorded. As shown in Figure 2i, the reproducible signals at different potentials indicated its operational stability. Incidentally, the behavior of the accumulation-mode OECT has been explained with Figure S7. Besides, the stability of the accumulation-mode OECT was studied as shown in Figure S8. According to our previous work [27], the redox species could influence the PEDOT:PSS channel, and the case study of this present work was carefully performed and discussed with Figure S9.
The as-developed $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ gate was then integrated with the DETA de-doped PEDOT:PSS to construct the proposed Signal-ON PEDOT:PSS OPECT biosensor, which was initially studied by recording $I_{\mathrm{G}}$ and $\mathrm{\Delta}I_{\mathrm{D}}$ responses in the absence and presence of the same $425\mathrm{nm}$ light irradiation. As shown in Figure 3a, a negligible constant $I_{\mathrm{G}}$ of ca. 3 nA was acquired in the dark state, which promptly increased to ca. $69\mathrm{nA}$ and finally reached ca. $24\mathrm{nA}$ after the action of light irradiation for $140\mathrm{~s~}$ (black line). Correspondingly, the $\boldsymbol{I_{\mathrm{D}}}$ exhibited gradual increase from ca. 200 to ca. $369\upmu\mathrm{A}$ (red line). The underlying rationale of this OPECT device was then proposed in Figure 3b. Without the light illumination, the PEDOT kept its reduced state with a low conductivity, whereas light-induced generation of negative photovoltage $(V_{\mathfrak{p}})$ would drive the ejection of cations out of and the injection of anions into the channel, rendering the recovery of PEDOT with increased conductivity as illustrated in Scheme 1d. Remarkably, a minute variation in $\Delta I_{\mathrm{G}}$ of ca. 21 nA could cause a great variation in $\Delta I_{\mathrm{D}}$ of $c a$ . $169\upmu\mathrm{A}$ . As calculated in Figure 3c, the current gain $G$ was as high as near ${10}^{4}$ , indicating the near ${10}^{4}$ times capability of this device to amplify the faradaic signal originating from specific biological event. To our knowledge, such a high gain is the highest one among existing OECT biosensory devices [9–15,46,47].
The biologically dependent modulation effect of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}{\bf C}_{2}/$ PCN-224/NiO gate was further studied. As displayed in Figure 3d, in the absence of light irradiation, the absence and presence of ALP generated two identical transfer characteristics (black and red solid lines), which, nevertheless, moved significantly to lower gate voltage in the presence of light irradiation (solid and dashed black lines). In detail, upon illumination, the transfer characteristics generated a greater offset in the presence of ALP-triggered bi-enzyme cascade system, as compared to the case of bare $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ gate (from black solid line to black dashed line). Figure S10
shows the relevant output characteristic curves, and the trend was in good agreement with the transfer characteristic curves. The corresponding mechanism from the perspective of potential distribution was depicted in Figure 3e. Originally, the potential was assigned to two solid-liquid interfaces with electric double layers (EDL), i.e., gate/electrolyte and electrolyte/channel, which could be considered as two capacitors of $C_{\mathrm{G}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{{C}}},$ respectively [48]. In the dark state, the potential drop on $C_{\mathrm{C}}$ was $\frac{V_{\mathrm{G}}}{1+\gamma}(\gamma=\frac{C_{\mathrm{C}}}{C_{\mathrm{G}}})$ , and it would be changed to $\frac{V_{\mathrm{G}}+V_{\mathrm{p}}}{1+\gamma}$ upon the light illumination [49]. The actual effective gate voltages $(V_{\mathrm{G}}^{\mathrm{eff}})$ could be described by the following equation:
$$
V_{\mathrm{~\scriptsize~G~}}^{\mathrm{~\scriptsize~eff}}{=}V_{\mathrm{\scriptsize~G}}{+}V_{\mathrm{\scriptsize~p}}
$$
In this sense, the photovoltages of $C_{\mathrm{G}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{C}}$ distributed by $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ were denoted as $V_{\Delta\mathrm{Gp}}$ and $V_{\Delta\mathrm{C-p}},$ respectively, which could induce the increase of the carrier concentration in de-doped PEDOT:PSS. In the presence of biologically generated quinones, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224/\mathrm{NiO}$ could result in a higher $V_{\mathrm{G}}^{\mathrm{eff-2}}$ , corresponding to higher $V_{\Delta\mathrm{G-p-b}}$ and $V_{\Delta\mathrm{C-p-b}}$ , leading to further enhanced conductivity of PEDOT: PSS.
Since the $I_{\mathrm{Dstep}}$ response was related to the ALP-dependent generation quinones, this OPECT device could be set up to detect ALP activity. As shown in Figure 3f, the $I_{\mathrm{D}\mathrm{step}}$ increased gradually with the increased ALP activity, implying the generation of more quinones and thus the higher $V_{\mathrm{G}}^{\mathrm{eff}}$ . Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ shows the derived calibration curve with a regression equation of $\Delta I/I=1.1952+0.2445\mathrm{log}C$ $R^{2}=0.9974)$ between 0.001 and $20\mathrm{~U~L~}^{-1}$ , and the detection limit of $0.001\ \mathrm{U~L~}^{-1}$ was experimentally determined, which was significantly lower than many recently reported studies, as listed in Table S1 (Supporting Information online). To study the selectivity of the biosensor, several common proteins such as horseradish peroxidase (HRP), albumin from bovine serum (BSA), lysozyme, and glucose oxidase (GOx) were employed. As depicted in Figure 3h, in the absence of ALP, the interfering substances led to negligible influence on ΔI/I (lines a–e), while the signals caused by the coexistence of ALP and interfering proteins were quite closed to that of bare ALP (lines f–j), indicating the good selectivity. Figure 3i shows the corresponding derived histogram.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we have devised a high-gain signal-on PEDOT: PSS OECT biosensing using a DETA de-doped PEDOT:PSS OECT modulated by a MXene/MOFs/NiO Schottky heterojunction. In such a system, the as-developed device achieved a remarkable current gain as high as near ${10}^{4}$ in response to the rational designed MXene/MOFs/NiO Schottky heterojunction using visible light as fuel. The superior modulation effect of MXene/MOFs/NiO could be attributed to the fast-directional charge transfer and the maximal accessibility of the electrolyte ensured by the 2D MXene with large surface area. Toward the detection of enzyme levels in a model ALP/TYR cascade system, highly sensitive ALP detection could be realized based on the biocatalytic generation of quinones that could efficiently promote the gating effect. This work not only provided a new perspective for development of signal-on PEDOT:PSS OPECT system with superior current gain, but also could serve as generic platform for advanced biomolecular detection.
